  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a southwest flow aloft prevailing across \nthe Western High Plains. Near the surface, a weak lee side \ntrough of low pressure remains anchored in eastern Colorado.\n\nHot/dry conditions will persist through late afternoon before \nminimal thunderstorm chances (30-50%) return this evening as the \nSREF indicates a closed upper low lifting northeast across the \nhigh plains of eastern Montana, helping nudge an anchored \nattendant lee side trough axis across extreme eastern Colorado \ninto west central Kansas by early evening. Prevailing \nsoutherlies will help reinforce pooled moisture ahead of the \ntrough axis with surface dewpoints holding in the mid/upper \n50s(F) to near 60F, providing sufficient instability with an \naxis of MUCAPE values in excess of 1500 to 2000 J/kg in vicinity\nof the trough axis. Despite marginal dynamic support aloft, \ndiurnally driven thunderstorm development is expected as H5 vort\nmaxima eject northeast out of the Southern Rockies, interacting\nwith the aforementioned trough axis/weak frontal boundary. The \nbest chance for rainfall will be across west central Kansas into\nextreme southwest Kansas as suggested by most CAMs and where \nthe HREF shows a 20 to 40% probability of 6-hr QPF exceeding 0.1\nof an inch by late Tuesday evening. Focus for thunderstorm \nchances (50-70%) shift farther southeast Wednesday with a weak \nfrontal boundary projected to push slowly through the remainder \nof southwest Kansas into south central Kansas by late afternoon.\n\nUnseasonably hot temperatures continue today as prevailing \nsoutherlies reinforce the much warmer air mass across the area \nwith H85 temperatures ranging from the upper 20s(C) in central \nKansas to a little above 30C in extreme southwest Kansas near \nthe Colorado line. Look for highs up into the lower/mid 90s(F) \nbehind the advancing trough axis in west central Kansas where \nthe latest HREF indicates a better than a 90% probability of \ntemperatures exceeding 90F but only a 50-70% probability of \ntopping 95F. Hotter temperatures are likely in south central \nKansas where HREF paints a 70-90% probability of highs nudging \nabove 100F. The run of temperatures climbing above the century \nmark ends for much of the area Wednesday in wake of a weak cold \nfront pushing farther south into the Texas Panhandle and western\nOklahoma, dropping H85 temperatures below 25C across southwest \nKansas into south central Kansas to even near 20C in central \nKansas. More seasonal temperatures are likely in west central \nKansas to parts of central and extreme southwest Kansas where \nthere is only a 20-40% probability of temperatures exceeding 85F\nto mainly the 90s(F) farther southeast into south central \nKansas.",
